A||||8||Greg Piet||Need helpful idea on possible oil leak locations||||1||
Z||000000||Greg Piet||11-13-2000||03:25 PM||||I am leaking oil somewhere around the harmonic balancer(I am currently stumped).<P>I have replaced the harmonic balancer and put in 2 new "speedi sleeves" with no fix.<BR>I have redone the oil pan gasket.<P>I cant find the friggin leak!<P>Where else around there would it be leaking?<BR>Maybe the oil pressure sensor line?<P>Note:  My oil pressure sensor is NEW and it it reading about 80psi at cold startup idle...  Is this possible high pressure so the speedi sleeve isnt able to stop the oil from coming out around the balancer?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-13-2000||06:16 PM||||Think 80psi at cold idle (by the gage) is normal.  Pressure falls off as car warms up.<P>If the oil leak coincided with installation of the OP sender, that's the first place to look.  Another possibility is drool from the interface between lower intake manifold and block, or even from the valve covers.<P>Sorry, not familiar with "speedi sleeves," so can't comment on that. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ponya||11-13-2000||06:44 PM||||Hi I had a similar problem, which actually still exists. I had a small drip problem and I thought it was the oil pan. Well I changed the gasket and it was still there. Since the leak is still in the same area, we are pretty sure it is the rear main seal. Not a fun project, that's why for now, I'll live with it. It will be fixed, just at a later date.||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||11-13-2000||06:58 PM||||Greg,Sometimes there is no easy way to find an oil leak.One of the best ways is to clean and dry the area around and above the oil you can see.Run the engine for a short time and check very carefully for any wet spots.If none are found after a few  minutes ,run and check again.It doesn't take much of a leak to look very bad.You may only see what looks like a wet spot,clean and dry again,make sure it's dry.If it gets wet again,youv'e found your leak.Hope this helps. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-13-2000||07:17 PM||||To narrow it a bit..... crank seals are not pressure seals. The oil that hits these seals is splash oil with little pressure.<P>I imagine you replaced the seal when you did the balancer. are you sure you didn't bend the timing cover or gouge the portion that holds the seal? either of those could cause a leak.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||11-13-2000||08:49 PM||||There are also some pretty neat ways to find where oil is coming out, and this is also how the service techs at GM garages may find a nagging leak.  There is a oil additive on the market that you put in and drive the car for a while, then, you use the UV light that comes with the kit, and shine it over the engine.  The oil that leaked out with the additive will "glow" when the UV is passed over it.  I think I may have seen it either at autozone, or possibly in a few catalogs, like jcwitney or summit racing.<P>------------------<BR>"The fish who keeps on swimming is the first to chill upstream"||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||11-14-2000||02:23 AM||||I think ponya has the same symptom as I do, btu I am not sure how the main seal can be the culprit.<P>If I am not mistaken, the main seal is behind the timing chain gear on the crank which in turn is behind the timing chain cover.<BR>The timing chain gets oil from my understanding and on the timing chain cover is the harmonic balancer seal(Speedi Sleve).<P>I even RTV/ed the speedi sleve after instalation around where it mates to the timing cover to ensure it was not leaking from there, but anyhows I did not  mark up the mating surfaces <P> So if the main seal is leaking it is just going to leak into an area where there is oil already, thus not a cause of a leak?<P>I am thinking the only option now is the timing cover gasket or the lower intake (I know the valve covers are OK)...||||reg||1
Z||000007||rodmcneill||11-14-2000||03:05 AM||||One thing to remember about oil leaks is that where the oil ends up at is not neccessarily where it came from.I had a quick oil change place tell me that my auto. trans.pan gskt. was leaking but it was actually motor oil. I thought it was the V6 dist.O ring so I replaced that. It was after I had pulled the dist. that I noticed that the leak was coming from behind it and was a missing piece of RTV sealant from the gap between the lower intake manifold and block.Oh well, the O ring probably needed replacing anyway!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Greg Piet||11-14-2000||05:11 AM||||Oh yeah I forgot to make sure<BR>The lower AC brackets bolt holes are not live are they?||||reg||1
